WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.540 --> 00:00:09.630
- Hello everyone, good afternoon.

00:00:09.630 --> 00:00:12.550
It gives me great
pleasure to invite you all

00:00:12.550 --> 00:00:15.680
to our discussion session,

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:20.170
a more in-depth discussion of
the talk we heard yesterday

00:00:20.170 --> 00:00:21.543
from Dr. Elise Crull.

00:00:22.410 --> 00:00:25.740
And as part of the
Inference Colloquium series,

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:29.060
as many of you know, we
have these two events.

00:00:29.060 --> 00:00:34.060
We have the talk followed
by a more open discussion

00:00:34.120 --> 00:00:37.060
with the issues that
were raised in the talk

00:00:37.060 --> 00:00:42.060
and yesterday Dr. Crull talked
to us about causal ordering

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:47.540
and in particular, the many
phony issues that come together

00:00:48.340 --> 00:00:51.920
when one is trying to
understand quantum entanglement

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:53.830
and the role of causal ordering

00:00:53.830 --> 00:00:57.560
in understanding quantum
mechanics more generally,

00:00:57.560 --> 00:01:00.450
and she presented some new ideas,

00:01:00.450 --> 00:01:05.450
new avenues that she's been
thinking about on this problem.

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:10.360
And it gives me great pleasure
today that our discussant

00:01:10.360 --> 00:01:12.163
and conversant with her,

00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:16.840
we have Professor Yemima Ben-Menahem

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:19.690
from the Department of Philosophy

00:01:19.690 --> 00:01:22.624
at Hebrew University of Jerusalem.

00:01:22.624 --> 00:01:27.020
Yemima Ben-Menahem is the
Barbara Druss Dibner Professor

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:29.230
of the History of Science Emeritus

00:01:29.230 --> 00:01:31.620
at the Hebrew University of Jerusalem

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:33.930
and a lecturer in the
Department of Philosophy

00:01:33.930 --> 00:01:36.253
and Jewish Thought, Shalem College.

00:01:37.460 --> 00:01:41.860
Professor Ben-Menahem, to
those of you who are familiar

00:01:41.860 --> 00:01:46.860
with the intellectual currents
and the philosophy of science

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:49.350
would know that needs no introduction.

00:01:49.350 --> 00:01:52.520
She's one of the preeminent
philosophers of science

00:01:52.520 --> 00:01:55.160
whose work has found causation,

00:01:55.160 --> 00:01:59.130
the interpretation of quantum
mechanics, scientific realism,

00:01:59.130 --> 00:02:03.050
the role of contingency
and necessity in history.

00:02:03.050 --> 00:02:05.180
So real broad pallet

00:02:05.180 --> 00:02:08.700
of really important and complex problems

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:10.103
that she has worked on.

00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:15.150
And so she is the perfect conversant

00:02:15.150 --> 00:02:18.090
from whom we can learn a lot

00:02:18.090 --> 00:02:22.780
about how to think about
these sort of radical new ways

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:24.780
that Elise presented yesterday

00:02:24.780 --> 00:02:28.173
for us to engage with the
concept of entanglement.

00:02:30.150 --> 00:02:32.220
Professor Ben-Menahem's recent book

00:02:32.220 --> 00:02:35.567
titled "Causation in
Science" published in 2018

00:02:36.674 --> 00:02:38.210
is truly remarkable.

00:02:38.210 --> 00:02:41.330
So if any one is looking for a primmer

00:02:41.330 --> 00:02:44.650
to get to speed on matters of causation

00:02:44.650 --> 00:02:46.710
in science, more broadly,

00:02:46.710 --> 00:02:49.560
including the many open
questions and debates,

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:52.090
I highly recommend the book.

00:02:52.090 --> 00:02:56.730
And before I turn the
floor over to Yemima,

00:02:56.730 --> 00:03:00.430
I would like to invite you and remind all

00:03:00.430 --> 00:03:02.680
to keep your video switched off.

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:05.600
And just a quick reminder on the format,

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:08.690
we will cede the floor
to Professor Ben-Menahem

00:03:08.690 --> 00:03:13.690
after which Elise and she will engage with

00:03:15.450 --> 00:03:19.270
going through for the points
of clarification and debate

00:03:19.270 --> 00:03:21.340
and then we open up to the floor

00:03:21.340 --> 00:03:23.240
for a general Q and A.

00:03:23.240 --> 00:03:26.370
So without further ado,

00:03:26.370 --> 00:03:29.630
I would like to turn it over to Yemima.

00:03:29.630 --> 00:03:30.913
Thank you so much.

00:03:31.950 --> 00:03:34.820
- Thank you very Priya for this invitation

00:03:34.820 --> 00:03:39.820
and thank you Ty and the
people who are here with us.

00:03:40.610 --> 00:03:45.610
And I'm really grateful
to Elise for introducing,

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:49.473
and I suppose many of us are,

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:55.030
for introducing us to
really cutting edge thinking

00:03:55.030 --> 00:03:58.697
in the foundations and experimentation

00:03:58.697 --> 00:04:02.040
and for the thinking and experimentation

00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:05.313
in the foundations of quantum mechanics.

00:04:09.340 --> 00:04:14.340
Actually I won't be
discussing superposition

00:04:15.567 --> 00:04:19.230
of causal states and processes

00:04:19.230 --> 00:04:22.160
that she presented to us yesterday,

00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:25.730
but I want to step back a little bit

00:04:25.730 --> 00:04:28.060
and go back first to

00:04:29.660 --> 00:04:32.480
a few words about quantum mechanics

00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:35.600
and then step back even further

00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:39.920
and talk a little more about causation.

00:04:39.920 --> 00:04:44.170
So quantum mechanics is mind-boggling,

00:04:44.170 --> 00:04:46.110
we all know that.

00:04:46.110 --> 00:04:49.380
And the price we have to pay,

00:04:49.380 --> 00:04:51.270
well, the benefits are enormous too,

00:04:51.270 --> 00:04:54.700
because it's the most
successful theory ever.

00:04:54.700 --> 00:04:59.360
But the intellectual price
we have to pay seems to rise

00:04:59.360 --> 00:05:03.880
with every new generation of
researchers looking into it.

00:05:03.880 --> 00:05:06.060
Because at the beginning we only thought,

00:05:06.060 --> 00:05:11.060
okay, Schrödinger's function
doesn't describe a wave

00:05:11.330 --> 00:05:13.250
in three dimensional space,

00:05:13.250 --> 00:05:16.100
it's a probability wave, okay.

00:05:16.100 --> 00:05:19.280
So maybe we have to give up determinism,

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:21.573
but that's not such a high price.

00:05:25.370 --> 00:05:26.420
But then it turned out

00:05:26.420 --> 00:05:29.420
that there are much more serious problems

00:05:29.420 --> 00:05:34.420
and one of them and perhaps
the most serious problem

00:05:35.100 --> 00:05:40.100
as Elise noted yesterday is
the problem of entanglement.

00:05:43.050 --> 00:05:45.967
And the reason it is
such an engaging problem

00:05:45.967 --> 00:05:47.640
and such a difficult problem

00:05:47.640 --> 00:05:51.810
is that it threatens
to get us into conflict

00:05:51.810 --> 00:05:53.820
with a special theory of relativity,

00:05:53.820 --> 00:05:57.303
which no one really wants to give up.

00:05:58.260 --> 00:06:01.530
So, this is the major problem.

00:06:01.530 --> 00:06:03.540
There are other problems,
the measurement problem,

00:06:03.540 --> 00:06:06.570
other problems, and there
are various solutions to it

00:06:06.570 --> 00:06:11.010
and none of them completely
satisfies everyone as yet.

00:06:11.010 --> 00:06:13.710
But I want to go back for a minute

00:06:13.710 --> 00:06:18.500
to entanglement and how it came...

00:06:18.500 --> 00:06:19.940
I have to...

00:06:19.940 --> 00:06:22.213
I thought I was sharing the screen.

00:06:23.090 --> 00:06:25.510
So we have the uncertainty principle

00:06:25.510 --> 00:06:30.390
and Einstein and his
colleagues, Podolsky and Rosen,

00:06:30.390 --> 00:06:32.620
was looking at this uncertainty principle

00:06:32.620 --> 00:06:37.130
which gives us a kind of
limit on what we can measure

00:06:37.130 --> 00:06:40.210
or how precisely we can prepare a system.

00:06:40.210 --> 00:06:42.900
But there was a camp in,

00:06:42.900 --> 00:06:45.510
well, mainly the Copenhagen Camp who said,

00:06:45.510 --> 00:06:48.490
no, this is a much more serious constraint

00:06:48.490 --> 00:06:50.943
and it has a limit on,

00:06:52.280 --> 00:06:55.290
not only at the precision
that we can measure something,

00:06:55.290 --> 00:06:58.340
not only at the precision
that we can prepare a system,

00:06:58.340 --> 00:07:02.340
but on the actual existence
of these variables.

00:07:02.340 --> 00:07:05.110
And there are certain pairs of variables.

00:07:05.110 --> 00:07:08.120
And initially, and we here too

00:07:08.120 --> 00:07:12.210
can talk about position and momentum.

00:07:12.210 --> 00:07:13.210
So what was...

00:07:13.210 --> 00:07:15.290
And if we have only one particle

00:07:15.290 --> 00:07:17.090
and we're going to measure its position,

00:07:17.090 --> 00:07:18.570
how are we going to prove

00:07:18.570 --> 00:07:21.280
that it didn't have a
momentum at the same time?

00:07:21.280 --> 00:07:22.190
We cannot do that

00:07:22.190 --> 00:07:24.820
because when we measure a
position as Heisenberg showed

00:07:24.820 --> 00:07:26.770
we interfere with the momentum

00:07:26.770 --> 00:07:29.370
and so we cannot prove that
the uncertainty principle

00:07:29.370 --> 00:07:33.170
is really such a strong
constraint on reality.

00:07:33.170 --> 00:07:36.983
So the ingenious idea
was to take this system

00:07:41.010 --> 00:07:44.790
and have two particles
instead of one particle.

00:07:44.790 --> 00:07:46.330
And then at the beginning,

00:07:46.330 --> 00:07:51.330
they start from a position
which we can designate by zero

00:07:53.960 --> 00:07:56.540
and zero momentum as well.

00:07:56.540 --> 00:07:59.220
And then there is some interactions

00:07:59.220 --> 00:08:03.840
that sends them into reverse directions.

00:08:03.840 --> 00:08:06.660
And now the story begins.

00:08:06.660 --> 00:08:09.340
We can either measure position

00:08:09.340 --> 00:08:11.360
and then the other party would know,

00:08:11.360 --> 00:08:15.730
and I'm specifically not
specifying at the moment why

00:08:15.730 --> 00:08:18.130
the other party would also know

00:08:18.130 --> 00:08:20.920
what position the particle is in,

00:08:20.920 --> 00:08:24.230
although no measurement was
carried out on the other side.

00:08:24.230 --> 00:08:27.020
But we could also measure momentum

00:08:27.020 --> 00:08:28.950
and the other party would know

00:08:28.950 --> 00:08:31.310
and since we didn't interfere

00:08:31.310 --> 00:08:33.503
with anything that the other party did,

00:08:34.750 --> 00:08:38.410
Einstein and colleagues concluded

00:08:38.410 --> 00:08:42.150
that both position and momentum exist

00:08:42.150 --> 00:08:44.680
and the only limitation of
the uncertainty principle

00:08:44.680 --> 00:08:46.873
is on measurement.

00:08:48.800 --> 00:08:52.110
Okay, why do I go to that problem?

00:08:52.110 --> 00:08:54.900
I go back to that problem
because already here,

00:08:54.900 --> 00:08:59.900
why is it that we assume that
the momentum of one particle

00:09:01.440 --> 00:09:04.423
has to be correlated with
it of the other particle,

00:09:05.730 --> 00:09:08.003
because there is a conservation law here.

00:09:09.130 --> 00:09:11.520
So already here we touch on something

00:09:11.520 --> 00:09:12.980
that according to my approach,

00:09:12.980 --> 00:09:17.980
and I will say more about it
later, is related to causality.

00:09:19.630 --> 00:09:22.890
Now, as thinking about that developed,

00:09:22.890 --> 00:09:24.320
a lot of things had to happen.

00:09:24.320 --> 00:09:26.460
First of all here we
have continuous variables

00:09:26.460 --> 00:09:29.410
then Bohm translated it
into discrete variables

00:09:29.410 --> 00:09:31.330
which would lead to measurement,

00:09:31.330 --> 00:09:35.300
then the famously Bell translated
it or not translated it,

00:09:35.300 --> 00:09:37.400
but reformulated the problem

00:09:37.400 --> 00:09:41.940
when we don't only measure
variables in one direction,

00:09:41.940 --> 00:09:44.210
but we play around with the correlations

00:09:44.210 --> 00:09:45.470
in different directions

00:09:45.470 --> 00:09:47.780
like three different spin measurements

00:09:47.780 --> 00:09:49.363
in different directions.

00:09:50.470 --> 00:09:55.050
And moreover, it turned out

00:09:55.050 --> 00:09:57.830
that we can send these particles,

00:09:57.830 --> 00:10:02.300
initially turned out that
in theory we could do that.

00:10:02.300 --> 00:10:04.450
We can send these particles

00:10:04.450 --> 00:10:07.220
so far away in space time that they are,

00:10:07.220 --> 00:10:09.230
as we say, spacetime related

00:10:09.230 --> 00:10:12.870
and they cannot interact with one another,

00:10:12.870 --> 00:10:15.750
according to the special
theory of relativity.

00:10:15.750 --> 00:10:18.900
And then the correlation
becomes really mysterious

00:10:18.900 --> 00:10:23.113
because how are we to
explain that correlation?

00:10:24.160 --> 00:10:26.030
They cannot signal to one another

00:10:26.030 --> 00:10:31.030
because we saw to it
that they're so far away.

00:10:31.160 --> 00:10:33.910
So the only other possibility is

00:10:33.910 --> 00:10:37.090
that there is a common
cause to this correlation.

00:10:37.090 --> 00:10:39.730
The only other possibility,

00:10:39.730 --> 00:10:41.740
given our classical way of thinking.

00:10:41.740 --> 00:10:43.000
When we have correlation,

00:10:43.000 --> 00:10:47.840
then either one is a cause of the other

00:10:47.840 --> 00:10:49.890
or the other is a cause of the first

00:10:49.890 --> 00:10:51.830
or if that cannot happen,

00:10:51.830 --> 00:10:55.100
if there cannot be a direct
connection between the two,

00:10:55.100 --> 00:10:59.620
we are looking for a common
cause for that correlation.

00:10:59.620 --> 00:11:03.770
And the big achievement
of Bell's inequalities

00:11:03.770 --> 00:11:08.620
was to rule out the possibility
of such a common cause.

00:11:08.620 --> 00:11:11.920
In this context we call the
common cause hidden variables,

00:11:11.920 --> 00:11:15.740
because supposedly there are
some additional variables

00:11:15.740 --> 00:11:20.230
that determine what would be
the result of a measurement

00:11:20.230 --> 00:11:21.710
on each one of the particles

00:11:21.710 --> 00:11:24.533
in each one of the directions
that we choose to measure.

00:11:25.940 --> 00:11:28.630
So, instead of common cause

00:11:28.630 --> 00:11:31.430
we are talking about hidden variables,

00:11:31.430 --> 00:11:33.433
but it amounts to the same thing.

00:11:36.030 --> 00:11:40.430
And so if there is no common cause

00:11:40.430 --> 00:11:43.740
then we're back to the
problem of correlation.

00:11:43.740 --> 00:11:46.683
So maybe they do signal to one another,

00:11:47.530 --> 00:11:50.630
but that would mean that we're violating

00:11:50.630 --> 00:11:53.120
the special theory of relativity.

00:11:53.120 --> 00:11:55.510
So this is a big conundrum,

00:11:55.510 --> 00:12:00.210
and as conundrums are sometimes solved,

00:12:00.210 --> 00:12:02.090
there is a distinction.

00:12:02.090 --> 00:12:05.010
These dates can be correlated,

00:12:05.010 --> 00:12:07.870
but they cannot signal to one another.

00:12:07.870 --> 00:12:10.970
And this is a new idea because, again,

00:12:10.970 --> 00:12:14.320
classically, we would
think that the correlation

00:12:14.320 --> 00:12:18.840
lets us or allows us to infer
from what happens to one,

00:12:18.840 --> 00:12:20.510
what happened to the other.

00:12:20.510 --> 00:12:25.510
But in this case we say
correlation, yes, signaling, no.

00:12:26.360 --> 00:12:29.670
So it's a new way of
understanding the correlation

00:12:29.670 --> 00:12:33.470
in order to save compatibility
between quantum mechanics

00:12:33.470 --> 00:12:35.880
and the special theory of relativity.

00:12:35.880 --> 00:12:40.150
Now, the especially theory of
relativity, as you remember,

00:12:40.150 --> 00:12:44.400
is a causal theory in a very strong sense

00:12:45.330 --> 00:12:47.760
because there is a limitation

00:12:47.760 --> 00:12:52.690
on how causal signals can travel.

00:12:52.690 --> 00:12:54.960
They can travel at just certain speed,

00:12:54.960 --> 00:12:57.460
not faster than the speed of light.

00:12:57.460 --> 00:13:02.460
And therefore it is causality
that divide spacetime

00:13:04.130 --> 00:13:08.240
into regions where events in those regions

00:13:08.240 --> 00:13:11.170
can interact with one another causally

00:13:11.170 --> 00:13:13.960
and regions that where events

00:13:13.960 --> 00:13:17.680
cannot interact with one another causally.

00:13:17.680 --> 00:13:22.680
And moreover, in those events,
in those areas of spacetime

00:13:24.130 --> 00:13:26.900
where there is no causal interaction,

00:13:26.900 --> 00:13:31.490
there is no well defined temporal order.

00:13:31.490 --> 00:13:33.980
So just in parenthesis,

00:13:33.980 --> 00:13:38.480
you remember you thought
he could define causation

00:13:38.480 --> 00:13:43.480
in terms of temporal order,
in terms of regularity

00:13:43.600 --> 00:13:48.600
and in a way special relativity
reverses the order and says,

00:13:49.940 --> 00:13:54.940
we know what a causal signal
is and how fast it can travel

00:13:55.260 --> 00:13:58.760
and that will tell us in
which areas of spacetime

00:13:58.760 --> 00:14:01.513
we can talk about the
definite temporal order.

00:14:07.970 --> 00:14:09.870
So, the problem of entanglement

00:14:09.870 --> 00:14:12.770
and the compatibility
problem that it raises

00:14:14.170 --> 00:14:18.560
confront us with two aspects of causation.

00:14:18.560 --> 00:14:21.870
One is that it is conservation laws

00:14:21.870 --> 00:14:25.070
that explain for us the correlation,

00:14:25.070 --> 00:14:28.680
we don't want to violate
certain conservation laws,

00:14:28.680 --> 00:14:32.860
and the other is that we
worry about the conflict

00:14:32.860 --> 00:14:36.300
with a theory which is
cause of through and through

00:14:36.300 --> 00:14:38.310
in a very strong sense.

00:14:38.310 --> 00:14:41.320
And as I will proceed to explain,

00:14:41.320 --> 00:14:45.060
I don't think there is one sense
to the notion of causation,

00:14:45.060 --> 00:14:47.680
but the theory of special relativity

00:14:47.680 --> 00:14:51.470
is a causal theory in a very strong sense.

00:14:51.470 --> 00:14:55.623
So, what can be done about this?

00:14:57.440 --> 00:15:00.503
So, a number of thinkers,

00:15:02.660 --> 00:15:06.630
I'm thinking primarily of
you Bryce, have come up,

00:15:06.630 --> 00:15:09.710
but he was not the first one
and he is not the only one

00:15:09.710 --> 00:15:11.020
and I have one of his...

00:15:11.020 --> 00:15:13.260
Has a nice formulation
of quantum mechanics

00:15:13.260 --> 00:15:17.320
that is similar to what Bryce had in mind

00:15:17.320 --> 00:15:20.660
and he's a physicist philosopher.

00:15:20.660 --> 00:15:23.170
So instead of talking about particles

00:15:23.170 --> 00:15:24.374
and their correlations,

00:15:24.374 --> 00:15:29.374
we can think analogously
about two twins and they're...

00:15:32.630 --> 00:15:34.480
This is, again, very familiar.

00:15:34.480 --> 00:15:39.100
It's not my translation
into this scenario,

00:15:39.100 --> 00:15:43.450
but they are presented
with a series of questions

00:15:43.450 --> 00:15:47.210
and we find that their questions...

00:15:47.210 --> 00:15:49.490
So somewhere in their future,

00:15:49.490 --> 00:15:52.660
there is someone asking
them these questions

00:15:52.660 --> 00:15:54.590
and we find that their answers

00:15:54.590 --> 00:15:57.410
are more correlated than we would expect

00:15:57.410 --> 00:16:00.630
if they'd had just answer randomly.

00:16:00.630 --> 00:16:04.510
And so we say, well, we have
here some kind of correlation,

00:16:04.510 --> 00:16:06.030
it's not random.

00:16:06.030 --> 00:16:07.840
There must be some common cause.

00:16:07.840 --> 00:16:12.670
So probably the twins have
kind of genetic property

00:16:12.670 --> 00:16:17.670
that ensures they're
replying to certain questions

00:16:18.880 --> 00:16:21.070
in a certain way.

00:16:21.070 --> 00:16:23.340
Now, according to what we just said,

00:16:23.340 --> 00:16:26.630
those inequalities and the
whole problem of entanglement

00:16:26.630 --> 00:16:30.710
is that if we assume such a common cause

00:16:30.710 --> 00:16:33.130
or we assume such hidden variables,

00:16:33.130 --> 00:16:35.990
we get into conflict
with quantum mechanics.

00:16:35.990 --> 00:16:39.280
And the violation of Bell's
inequalities were confirmed

00:16:39.280 --> 00:16:44.280
by experience many, many
times since the eighties.

00:16:44.740 --> 00:16:47.200
So if that's not a possibility,

00:16:47.200 --> 00:16:51.443
if we cannot go on the left
schema of a common cause

00:16:54.060 --> 00:16:56.970
to the correlation between the two twins,

00:16:56.970 --> 00:17:01.330
their new prices, why don't
we go in the other direction

00:17:01.330 --> 00:17:05.920
and suppose that by
asking these questions,

00:17:05.920 --> 00:17:10.920
the questioner causes
or acts upon the twins,

00:17:16.020 --> 00:17:19.980
making them, giving them the property

00:17:19.980 --> 00:17:24.980
that will yield the
correlation between them.

00:17:25.360 --> 00:17:28.630
So rather than looking for
a common cause in the past,

00:17:28.630 --> 00:17:31.170
we look at something in the future

00:17:31.170 --> 00:17:36.170
that who's influence goes back
and explains the correlation.

00:17:36.550 --> 00:17:39.430
So this of course is a very radical idea

00:17:39.430 --> 00:17:43.710
because we haven't encountered
anything like retrocausation

00:17:46.300 --> 00:17:48.240
in our daily lives.

00:17:48.240 --> 00:17:52.790
And so this possibility sits there

00:17:52.790 --> 00:17:54.780
and I want to ask later

00:17:54.780 --> 00:17:59.780
whether one takes it to be
only an epistemic possibility.

00:18:01.100 --> 00:18:03.310
If we know what the answer was,

00:18:03.310 --> 00:18:06.340
we could go back and
calculate probabilities,

00:18:06.340 --> 00:18:08.960
or if this is supposed to be something

00:18:08.960 --> 00:18:10.873
about physical reality.

00:18:14.190 --> 00:18:17.760
But anyway, it's a very radical solution.

00:18:17.760 --> 00:18:20.930
And what we heard yesterday

00:18:20.930 --> 00:18:25.930
is certainly a kind of spinoff

00:18:26.610 --> 00:18:31.610
from that route of that
approach where we say,

00:18:35.530 --> 00:18:40.530
look, why are we so tied
to the classical picture

00:18:41.340 --> 00:18:43.890
where the past influences the future,

00:18:43.890 --> 00:18:46.570
but the future never influences the past.

00:18:46.570 --> 00:18:49.773
Perhaps we should give up this assumption.

00:18:50.670 --> 00:18:54.270
So should we give up causal asymmetry

00:18:54.270 --> 00:18:56.750
or temporary asymmetry?

00:18:56.750 --> 00:18:59.330
Because the two are related and as I said,

00:18:59.330 --> 00:19:03.860
sometimes we see causation is
parasitic on temporal order

00:19:03.860 --> 00:19:05.420
and sometimes it's the other way around.

00:19:05.420 --> 00:19:07.913
There's in special relativity, I think.

00:19:10.900 --> 00:19:15.900
So according to Bryce, according
to some other philosophers

00:19:16.660 --> 00:19:21.610
and according to Elise yesterday,

00:19:21.610 --> 00:19:25.500
this is indeed a lesson
which we should draw.

00:19:25.500 --> 00:19:29.530
And her approach wasn't
based on Bryce's argument

00:19:29.530 --> 00:19:31.350
or on retrocausality,

00:19:31.350 --> 00:19:36.350
but it was based on these
very recent arguments,

00:19:36.640 --> 00:19:39.940
theoretical arguments and
very recent experiments

00:19:39.940 --> 00:19:41.583
that seem to suggest,

00:19:43.007 --> 00:19:46.000
I almost say demonstrate the experiment,

00:19:46.000 --> 00:19:49.690
but let me be very
careful, seem to suggest

00:19:49.690 --> 00:19:54.363
that this is really a very
fruitful approach to take.

00:19:56.770 --> 00:19:59.810
And I want to emphasize that the idea here

00:19:59.810 --> 00:20:04.360
to relinquish the causal
order between past and future

00:20:04.360 --> 00:20:09.360
applies both according to
Bryce and according to Elise

00:20:10.880 --> 00:20:13.440
to the fundamental level of physics.

00:20:13.440 --> 00:20:17.047
And there're other philosophers

00:20:17.047 --> 00:20:19.210
who've argued in that direction

00:20:19.210 --> 00:20:22.030
and I think even Carlo Roveli,

00:20:22.030 --> 00:20:23.920
the first lecture of the series

00:20:23.920 --> 00:20:27.113
which I listened to with great pleasure.

00:20:28.190 --> 00:20:30.480
So we're talking about the
fundamental level of physics

00:20:30.480 --> 00:20:34.469
and say, here, there is no
temporal direction or no,

00:20:34.469 --> 00:20:37.690
or no causal direction.

00:20:37.690 --> 00:20:41.410
And the question of why
we get the impression

00:20:41.410 --> 00:20:43.230
that there is such a thing

00:20:43.230 --> 00:20:45.880
on higher level is a very big question.

00:20:45.880 --> 00:20:48.550
And I think we should get to that question

00:20:48.550 --> 00:20:50.113
in the discussion.

00:20:51.870 --> 00:20:53.500
So this is one thing I want to note

00:20:53.500 --> 00:20:55.280
about yesterday's suggestion

00:20:55.280 --> 00:20:57.280
and the other thing is more important

00:20:58.780 --> 00:21:01.280
because you see there was this argument

00:21:01.280 --> 00:21:03.700
for years and years and years,

00:21:03.700 --> 00:21:07.260
that the fundamental laws of nature

00:21:08.550 --> 00:21:11.050
are time reversal symmetric.

00:21:11.050 --> 00:21:14.610
They don't distinguish the
fundamental laws of nature,

00:21:14.610 --> 00:21:18.110
do not distinguish
between past and future.

00:21:18.110 --> 00:21:21.563
No error of time at the
fundamental level of physics.

00:21:22.440 --> 00:21:26.260
But I always felt that there
was a gap in this argument

00:21:26.260 --> 00:21:30.590
and to conclude from the
time reversal symmetry

00:21:30.590 --> 00:21:32.610
of the fundamental laws,

00:21:32.610 --> 00:21:37.610
that there is no temporal
order in individual processes,

00:21:37.900 --> 00:21:41.610
seemed to me a big jump,
because it couldn't be

00:21:41.610 --> 00:21:44.440
that all processes can go from
one direction to the other

00:21:44.440 --> 00:21:46.790
and also from the other
direction to the first.

00:21:48.130 --> 00:21:51.780
Like we can blow a balloon
and fills up with air

00:21:51.780 --> 00:21:53.577
and then we deflate it

00:21:53.577 --> 00:21:58.020
and it's, again, this
little piece of rubber

00:21:58.020 --> 00:22:00.270
and both can happen,

00:22:00.270 --> 00:22:04.320
but in each individual case
we could think, supposedly,

00:22:04.320 --> 00:22:07.570
in each individual case we can say,

00:22:07.570 --> 00:22:11.370
by pointing to initial conditions
of whatever we could say,

00:22:11.370 --> 00:22:14.880
what the direction in
this individual case was.

00:22:14.880 --> 00:22:17.430
So it seemed to me that there
is a gap in the argument

00:22:17.430 --> 00:22:22.430
from time reversal symmetry
of the fundamental laws

00:22:22.710 --> 00:22:26.150
to no temporal order on
the fundamental level

00:22:26.150 --> 00:22:28.200
or no causal order on the entry level.

00:22:28.200 --> 00:22:31.750
And what I found so fascinating

00:22:31.750 --> 00:22:36.750
about the results that
Elise presented yesterday

00:22:36.920 --> 00:22:39.923
was that here we talking
about individual processes.

00:22:41.150 --> 00:22:44.520
We're not rehearsing this older argument

00:22:44.520 --> 00:22:46.233
about the fundamental laws.

00:22:47.460 --> 00:22:52.460
And yet, we should note that
the asymmetry in question

00:22:55.240 --> 00:22:58.850
is but one of the characteristics

00:22:58.850 --> 00:23:02.463
traditionally associated
with the notion of cause.

00:23:04.970 --> 00:23:09.283
So even if we give up this asymmetry,

00:23:12.560 --> 00:23:17.560
this doesn't mean that we
give up causation in general.

00:23:17.690 --> 00:23:22.690
So giving up the asymmetry
of the causal relations,

00:23:23.870 --> 00:23:26.500
a temporal asymmetry
of the causal relation,

00:23:26.500 --> 00:23:31.500
doesn't mean that we must
give up the notion of cause.

00:23:31.500 --> 00:23:33.653
And here I get to my first question.

00:23:35.630 --> 00:23:40.347
So, into both(chuckles),
imagine it's causation.

00:23:42.213 --> 00:23:45.700
I know Mark Twain speaking to you

00:23:45.700 --> 00:23:48.820
and the reports of the death of causation

00:23:48.820 --> 00:23:52.800
are greatly exaggerated on my view.

00:23:52.800 --> 00:23:54.200
So why is that?

00:23:54.200 --> 00:23:55.103
Can you hear me?

00:23:58.410 --> 00:23:59.243
- Yep.

00:24:00.310 --> 00:24:03.530
- So here comes my first quick question

00:24:03.530 --> 00:24:05.100
and it goes to you Elise,

00:24:05.100 --> 00:24:07.830
but it goes to other people
who've written about it.

00:24:07.830 --> 00:24:10.910
Are we talking about the
principle of causality?

00:24:10.910 --> 00:24:12.530
I think you mentioned such a principle

00:24:12.530 --> 00:24:14.240
in your talk yesterday,

00:24:14.240 --> 00:24:17.193
or are we talking about
the concept of cause?

00:24:19.270 --> 00:24:22.160
And so just to show you
that people confuse it too,

00:24:22.160 --> 00:24:26.230
and later in the discussion
maybe I could explain why.

00:24:26.230 --> 00:24:27.280
So here's Russell.

00:24:27.280 --> 00:24:31.130
The word cause is so inextricably bound up

00:24:31.130 --> 00:24:33.420
with misleading association

00:24:33.420 --> 00:24:36.720
as to make it's complete extrusion

00:24:36.720 --> 00:24:41.720
from the philosophical
vocabulary desirable.

00:24:42.010 --> 00:24:46.320
And that is after criticizing
the principle of causation.

00:24:46.320 --> 00:24:49.063
So he concludes, no principle no concept.

00:24:51.380 --> 00:24:55.363
And much more recently,
Joe Norton, of course says,

00:24:57.210 --> 00:24:58.610
centuries of failed attempts

00:24:58.610 --> 00:25:01.150
to formulate a principle of causality,

00:25:01.150 --> 00:25:03.550
robustly true under the introduction

00:25:03.550 --> 00:25:05.130
of new scientific theories,

00:25:05.130 --> 00:25:08.850
have left the notion
of causation so plastic

00:25:08.850 --> 00:25:11.010
that virtually any new science

00:25:11.010 --> 00:25:13.290
can be made to conform with it.

00:25:13.290 --> 00:25:16.750
So I think it's a big exaggeration.

00:25:16.750 --> 00:25:17.930
It's not so plastic.

00:25:17.930 --> 00:25:22.000
It's not so easy to construct a new theory

00:25:22.000 --> 00:25:27.000
that follows constraints
that you wanted to follow

00:25:27.060 --> 00:25:28.660
or to satisfy.

00:25:28.660 --> 00:25:31.240
But what I pointed here

00:25:31.240 --> 00:25:35.233
is a confusion between the
principle and the notion.

00:25:37.770 --> 00:25:41.990
And I don't know what the
principle of causality is

00:25:43.610 --> 00:25:46.730
in the sense that there are
at least two formulations

00:25:46.730 --> 00:25:50.850
of the principle of causality
and they're not synonymous

00:25:50.850 --> 00:25:54.620
and they're not even co-extensional.

00:25:54.620 --> 00:25:58.530
So, but again, I skipped
that and I can answer.

00:25:58.530 --> 00:26:02.760
I can explain why I'm saying
that later in the discussion.

00:26:02.760 --> 00:26:07.367
But anyway, I don't want
to suppose that we endorse

00:26:08.760 --> 00:26:12.833
or uphold the principle of causality.

00:26:14.290 --> 00:26:19.290
What I do want to suppose
or to propose, well, first.

00:26:21.200 --> 00:26:23.240
So now there are many
definitions of causation.

00:26:23.240 --> 00:26:25.470
There is a vast philosophical literature.

00:26:25.470 --> 00:26:27.150
People are working on causation

00:26:27.150 --> 00:26:29.010
and whether it's a human definition,

00:26:29.010 --> 00:26:31.150
whether it's David Lewis's definition,

00:26:31.150 --> 00:26:33.620
whether it's energy transfer,

00:26:33.620 --> 00:26:36.630
whether it's a probabilistic
definition of causation,

00:26:36.630 --> 00:26:40.270
many competing definitions in the field,

00:26:40.270 --> 00:26:44.440
but they all refer to the causal relations

00:26:44.440 --> 00:26:46.663
between individual events.

00:26:47.730 --> 00:26:49.110
And each one has its problems

00:26:49.110 --> 00:26:50.880
and each one can be patched up,

00:26:50.880 --> 00:26:55.880
but I don't want to follow
this well-trodden route.

00:26:57.580 --> 00:26:59.203
What do I want to suggest?

00:27:00.040 --> 00:27:02.590
I suggest, I have suggested it already,

00:27:02.590 --> 00:27:04.850
I cannot pretend that it just occurred

00:27:04.850 --> 00:27:07.630
to me yesterday after Elise's talk.

00:27:07.630 --> 00:27:12.630
So I suggest looking instead
at more general constraints,

00:27:13.520 --> 00:27:17.923
which in my view comprise
the notion of cause.

00:27:19.840 --> 00:27:24.063
So what is a causal constraint in my view?

00:27:26.040 --> 00:27:30.270
A causal constraint is
a constraint on change.

00:27:30.270 --> 00:27:34.733
It tells us which changes
and processes are possible

00:27:37.080 --> 00:27:42.080
or even necessary, and no
less important, in my view,

00:27:42.160 --> 00:27:45.053
which changes are impossible.

00:27:46.580 --> 00:27:51.070
And if you go back to the
philosophical literature

00:27:51.070 --> 00:27:55.960
about causation, for example,
Woodward interventions,

00:27:55.960 --> 00:27:57.100
this sort of thing,

00:27:57.100 --> 00:27:59.630
then they're often very good

00:27:59.630 --> 00:28:02.273
at explaining why something happened,

00:28:03.190 --> 00:28:04.470
but they're not so good

00:28:04.470 --> 00:28:07.310
at explaining why something didn't happen.

00:28:07.310 --> 00:28:10.280
And I think that in
science it's crucial for us

00:28:11.320 --> 00:28:14.590
when we think that something can happen,

00:28:14.590 --> 00:28:16.370
but we never find it.

00:28:16.370 --> 00:28:20.133
We never find two electrons
in the same level.

00:28:21.300 --> 00:28:24.010
We want to know why.

00:28:24.010 --> 00:28:25.940
And it's a methodological principle

00:28:25.940 --> 00:28:29.240
that we should find a law or a constraint

00:28:30.330 --> 00:28:35.330
that excludes this option
that we thought was possible,

00:28:36.870 --> 00:28:38.920
but we don't find it.

00:28:38.920 --> 00:28:42.400
And that's why I think the
language of causal constraint

00:28:42.400 --> 00:28:45.510
is much better to explain
what doesn't happen.

00:28:45.510 --> 00:28:48.410
And I think it's crucial for science

00:28:48.410 --> 00:28:50.640
to think both about what happens

00:28:50.640 --> 00:28:52.612
and about what doesn't happen.

00:28:52.612 --> 00:28:55.260
- So, Yemima, I just have
a quick clarification.

00:28:55.260 --> 00:29:00.260
So a causal constraint
then by this definition

00:29:00.570 --> 00:29:03.773
is intimately related to the
idea of the counterfactual.

00:29:05.530 --> 00:29:06.593
Right or?

00:29:10.310 --> 00:29:12.970
- It is but you know,

00:29:12.970 --> 00:29:15.980
this is not the basis of the definition

00:29:15.980 --> 00:29:19.760
because there are definitions
of causation(indistinct).

00:29:19.760 --> 00:29:21.540
A cause is a necessary condition,

00:29:21.540 --> 00:29:23.280
which means that if it hadn't happened,

00:29:23.280 --> 00:29:25.020
the effect wouldn't have happened.

00:29:25.020 --> 00:29:25.950
But I'm not saying that.

00:29:25.950 --> 00:29:28.658
I'm saying a conservation
law is a causal constraint.

00:29:28.658 --> 00:29:30.770
(indistinct)

00:29:30.770 --> 00:29:35.770
So, yes, if we wouldn't have
the conservation of energy,

00:29:36.090 --> 00:29:38.340
we could build a perpetual mobile machine.

00:29:38.340 --> 00:29:42.600
So almost everything we say
supports some counterfactuals.

00:29:42.600 --> 00:29:44.100
We say something positive

00:29:44.100 --> 00:29:46.540
and it supports some counterfactuals.

00:29:46.540 --> 00:29:48.007
And I'm for counterfactuals,

00:29:48.007 --> 00:29:50.435
I have no problem with
counterfactuals, even in history,

00:29:50.435 --> 00:29:53.150
but this is not the definition.

00:29:53.150 --> 00:29:55.360
This is not the defining characteristic.

00:29:55.360 --> 00:29:57.780
But perhaps we should pause for a moment

00:29:57.780 --> 00:29:59.523
on this notion of change,

00:30:01.190 --> 00:30:03.110
because you know from antiquity,

00:30:03.110 --> 00:30:04.860
philosophers have argued like

00:30:04.860 --> 00:30:07.460
for many just saying there's
no change in our guide

00:30:07.460 --> 00:30:08.730
to saying everything is changed.

00:30:08.730 --> 00:30:11.660
You cannot step into the same river twice.

00:30:11.660 --> 00:30:14.830
And we today have a kind of sense of this

00:30:14.830 --> 00:30:16.890
between those views.

00:30:16.890 --> 00:30:19.870
We say, yes, there is change in the world,

00:30:19.870 --> 00:30:22.460
but not any change.

00:30:22.460 --> 00:30:25.380
We have constraints on change.

00:30:25.380 --> 00:30:28.380
Why do I think that this
is important for physics?

00:30:28.380 --> 00:30:30.310
Because unlike mathematics,

00:30:30.310 --> 00:30:35.100
there're mathematical constraints
in physics, of course.

00:30:35.100 --> 00:30:37.060
But the physical constraints

00:30:37.060 --> 00:30:39.290
that are not mathematical in my view

00:30:39.290 --> 00:30:41.230
are constraints on change.

00:30:41.230 --> 00:30:43.880
Mathematics doesn't
have a notion of change.

00:30:43.880 --> 00:30:46.560
And so I think everything
that governs change,

00:30:46.560 --> 00:30:48.990
don't take the metaphor of
governing too literally,

00:30:48.990 --> 00:30:52.440
but everything that tells us
which changes are possible

00:30:52.440 --> 00:30:56.300
and which changes are not
possible has to do with causation.

00:30:56.300 --> 00:30:58.480
Now, I know that I'm vulnerable here

00:30:58.480 --> 00:31:03.150
to what Quine used to say,

00:31:03.150 --> 00:31:06.800
by denying the doctrine one
only changes the subject.

00:31:06.800 --> 00:31:08.250
So in a way change the subject

00:31:08.250 --> 00:31:10.620
because I'm not talking
only about causal relations

00:31:10.620 --> 00:31:14.010
between events and I want
to broaden the picture,

00:31:14.010 --> 00:31:19.010
but I think I have motivated
my wish to broaden the picture

00:31:21.890 --> 00:31:24.380
and I'll say more about it in a minute.

00:31:24.380 --> 00:31:26.080
So examples of constraints

00:31:26.080 --> 00:31:29.890
and I want to emphasize that
none of them is a priori,

00:31:29.890 --> 00:31:32.340
we have to check in each theory,

00:31:32.340 --> 00:31:36.620
whether the theory
satisfies this constraint

00:31:36.620 --> 00:31:38.210
and we check it empirically.

00:31:38.210 --> 00:31:40.850
So we don't impose our constraints.

00:31:40.850 --> 00:31:45.850
But there're constraints
that have gained our presence

00:31:47.130 --> 00:31:51.200
and we rely on them and we use
them to build new theories.

00:31:51.200 --> 00:31:56.200
There are symmetries that we
use to construct new theories.

00:31:56.670 --> 00:31:58.230
We trust the symmetry

00:31:58.230 --> 00:32:03.230
and we think that our theory
should be generally covariant,

00:32:03.650 --> 00:32:05.860
or we think it should follow

00:32:05.860 --> 00:32:09.140
some other symmetries principle
of relativity or whatever.

00:32:09.140 --> 00:32:11.280
So here are some examples of constraints.

00:32:11.280 --> 00:32:16.280
Determinism, locality.

00:32:16.530 --> 00:32:21.200
And, again, I emphasize
they can be disproved.

00:32:21.200 --> 00:32:23.930
Maybe the fundamental theories
are not deterministic.

00:32:23.930 --> 00:32:27.090
Maybe some theories are,
some theories are not,

00:32:27.090 --> 00:32:30.110
but there are constraints
that have been used

00:32:30.110 --> 00:32:31.630
and that we should consider

00:32:31.630 --> 00:32:36.630
and that are part of the
cluster notion of cause.

00:32:38.930 --> 00:32:40.350
The other one is locality.

00:32:40.350 --> 00:32:42.160
We've talked about this at the beginning

00:32:42.160 --> 00:32:43.660
and yesterday, of course.

00:32:43.660 --> 00:32:46.960
There're symmetries and conservation laws,

00:32:46.960 --> 00:32:47.793
which are very important.

00:32:47.793 --> 00:32:50.900
They are high up in the
hierarchy of physics.

00:32:50.900 --> 00:32:52.860
There's Curie's principle,

00:32:52.860 --> 00:32:56.060
which tells us that every
symmetry in the cause

00:32:56.060 --> 00:32:59.490
has to be in the effect,
but it uses causal notion,

00:32:59.490 --> 00:33:04.110
so we can formulate it in terms
of equations and solutions.

00:33:04.110 --> 00:33:06.420
We can formulate it in various other ways

00:33:06.420 --> 00:33:09.300
that do not presuppose
the notion of course.

00:33:09.300 --> 00:33:11.870
We have invariance
principles which are, again,

00:33:11.870 --> 00:33:15.230
very high up in the hierarchy
of physics symmetries,

00:33:15.230 --> 00:33:16.540
and invariance principles

00:33:16.540 --> 00:33:18.100
and there is a connection between them,

00:33:18.100 --> 00:33:19.400
but we don't go into that.

00:33:22.060 --> 00:33:24.880
And stability sometimes, not always.

00:33:24.880 --> 00:33:25.840
It's very important.

00:33:25.840 --> 00:33:27.250
I think the notion of stability,

00:33:27.250 --> 00:33:29.380
like the notion of
necessity and contingency

00:33:29.380 --> 00:33:30.980
is very important in human life.

00:33:30.980 --> 00:33:33.810
Again, I'm not stopping to
discuss this notion here,

00:33:33.810 --> 00:33:38.090
but just remember that
when Bohr was struggling

00:33:38.090 --> 00:33:41.560
to construct a new model of the atom,

00:33:41.560 --> 00:33:44.960
he had to comply with
what we know about atoms,

00:33:44.960 --> 00:33:46.560
that they are very, very stable

00:33:46.560 --> 00:33:49.440
and the classical model
wouldn't give him the stability

00:33:49.440 --> 00:33:52.560
and therefore he had to assume
all kinds of assumptions

00:33:52.560 --> 00:33:55.010
about stationary state,
they do not radiate,

00:33:55.010 --> 00:33:56.220
et cetera, et cetera.

00:33:56.220 --> 00:34:00.233
So sometimes stability is
also of course a constraint.

00:34:01.247 --> 00:34:04.760
And these constraints I've
dealt with in my book,

00:34:04.760 --> 00:34:09.280
but there are other
constraints that are...

00:34:10.980 --> 00:34:13.010
In any way I didn't think in the book

00:34:13.010 --> 00:34:15.670
that I had something very
new to say about them

00:34:15.670 --> 00:34:18.580
and I was also indecisive about them

00:34:18.580 --> 00:34:20.650
and one of them is causal asymmetry.

00:34:20.650 --> 00:34:23.270
So one reason I didn't want
to spend much time on it

00:34:23.270 --> 00:34:27.800
in the book was that Mathias
Frisch just published,

00:34:27.800 --> 00:34:29.880
a year or two years earlier,

00:34:29.880 --> 00:34:34.090
a book entirely dedicated
to causal asymmetry,

00:34:34.090 --> 00:34:38.210
and the other reason
was that, I don't know,

00:34:38.210 --> 00:34:40.670
I think it's too open at the moment.

00:34:40.670 --> 00:34:42.470
How much of a constraint is it?

00:34:42.470 --> 00:34:45.020
It's not something that usually

00:34:46.210 --> 00:34:48.090
would be considered a constraint

00:34:48.090 --> 00:34:51.780
that would tell us what kind
of theory we should construct.

00:34:51.780 --> 00:34:53.880
It's not like the principle of relativity.

00:34:53.880 --> 00:34:55.760
It's not like least action.

00:34:55.760 --> 00:34:59.870
It's not like these
well-established principles.

00:34:59.870 --> 00:35:04.790
And yesterday we heard
of very recent reasons

00:35:04.790 --> 00:35:08.750
of why it's not just such
a fundamental principle.

00:35:08.750 --> 00:35:10.750
And there is another interesting principle

00:35:10.750 --> 00:35:14.380
and it may even be somehow
related to this principle,

00:35:14.380 --> 00:35:17.530
which is a mutuality of causal action,

00:35:17.530 --> 00:35:20.630
which means that if here we
are talking about events,

00:35:20.630 --> 00:35:24.490
if the cause act on the effect,

00:35:24.490 --> 00:35:26.300
the effect also act on the cause

00:35:26.300 --> 00:35:29.253
and you could say this
is Newton's third law.

00:35:31.632 --> 00:35:34.170
And in that case it's
certainly a causal constraint,

00:35:34.170 --> 00:35:38.567
but Einstein used it as a justification.

00:35:41.270 --> 00:35:44.100
Even if it was a
justification after the fact

00:35:44.100 --> 00:35:46.943
for justifying dynamical spacetime.

00:35:48.500 --> 00:35:51.620
Because he said there was
something very unsatisfactory,

00:35:51.620 --> 00:35:55.160
causally unsatisfactory in Newton theory.

00:35:55.160 --> 00:35:58.380
Because here in Newton
theory we have space

00:35:58.380 --> 00:36:01.060
and space act on matter

00:36:01.060 --> 00:36:06.050
because acceleration relative
to space has physical meaning.

00:36:06.050 --> 00:36:09.200
But we don't have matter
acting back on space.

00:36:09.200 --> 00:36:11.285
And in my theory, in general relativity,

00:36:11.285 --> 00:36:14.210
we have matter acting back on space

00:36:14.210 --> 00:36:16.580
so this restores the symmetry.

00:36:16.580 --> 00:36:19.940
So this was the line of
argument in Einstein in 22,

00:36:19.940 --> 00:36:24.240
wasn't mentioned in 1922,

00:36:24.240 --> 00:36:26.863
wasn't mentioned in the 1916 paper.

00:36:27.740 --> 00:36:30.350
So it may be something that
occurred to him afterwards,

00:36:30.350 --> 00:36:32.477
but again, it's a kind of constraint.

00:36:32.477 --> 00:36:37.477
Sometimes we think it's
a marriage of a theory

00:36:38.750 --> 00:36:41.650
if it's a "basic, but it's not
something that fundamental.

00:36:44.430 --> 00:36:47.710
Okay. So why the causal
constraints approach?

00:36:47.710 --> 00:36:50.290
Why do I prefer it,
although it has this problem

00:36:50.290 --> 00:36:52.620
of changing the subject somewhat?

00:36:52.620 --> 00:36:54.960
So first of all, I think
this is how scientists think.

00:36:54.960 --> 00:36:56.950
I think very rarely scientists think

00:36:56.950 --> 00:37:01.630
about the case of two people
trying to murder a third one

00:37:01.630 --> 00:37:05.420
and one punches a hole in the water flask

00:37:05.420 --> 00:37:06.970
and the other one puts poison in it

00:37:06.970 --> 00:37:09.130
and then whose responsible
et cetera et cetera.

00:37:09.130 --> 00:37:11.680
This is not the kind of question
that worries scientists,

00:37:11.680 --> 00:37:13.770
but scientists want to worry...

00:37:13.770 --> 00:37:17.740
They want to know about basic symmetries,

00:37:17.740 --> 00:37:19.410
basic invariance laws.

00:37:19.410 --> 00:37:23.040
So I think there are more
useful to think about

00:37:23.040 --> 00:37:25.140
in the context of scientific reasoning.

00:37:25.140 --> 00:37:27.350
There're also historical reasons,

00:37:27.350 --> 00:37:30.153
because if you think of
it, conservation laws,

00:37:33.080 --> 00:37:37.850
there were ancient ideas
about not creating anything

00:37:37.850 --> 00:37:40.430
for X helio, so to speak,

00:37:40.430 --> 00:37:44.400
and that nothing gets
lost back into the void.

00:37:44.400 --> 00:37:46.470
So there was this idea of conservation.

00:37:46.470 --> 00:37:51.470
There was an idea that the
cause equals the effect.

00:37:51.510 --> 00:37:55.870
So in some sense they're historical root

00:37:55.870 --> 00:38:00.680
to the idea that conservation
laws are causal constraints

00:38:00.680 --> 00:38:04.701
and you have people like
Mason and even Bohr.

00:38:04.701 --> 00:38:08.700
When Bohr talks about causality,

00:38:08.700 --> 00:38:11.520
he actually talks about conservation laws.

00:38:11.520 --> 00:38:13.890
And, again, I won't go into it here

00:38:13.890 --> 00:38:16.610
but at least probably knows
because she writes a book

00:38:16.610 --> 00:38:17.550
or she has written a book

00:38:17.550 --> 00:38:20.950
on the history of quantum mechanics

00:38:20.950 --> 00:38:23.130
and other philosophers,

00:38:23.130 --> 00:38:24.990
like, I mean Madison also thinks

00:38:24.990 --> 00:38:26.220
the conservation laws are very,

00:38:26.220 --> 00:38:27.913
so there are historical reasons.

00:38:29.170 --> 00:38:31.260
There are, as I said before,

00:38:31.260 --> 00:38:34.310
causal constraints explain
what we do not find

00:38:34.310 --> 00:38:38.410
and that's an function
of a causal constraints,

00:38:38.410 --> 00:38:41.610
but the fourth reason for me,

00:38:41.610 --> 00:38:45.900
and this does not depend
on changing the subject,

00:38:45.900 --> 00:38:48.290
it's not a semantic issue

00:38:48.290 --> 00:38:51.230
and that this approach
of the causal constraints

00:38:51.230 --> 00:38:53.100
opens a variety of questions

00:38:53.100 --> 00:38:57.140
about the relations between
different constraints.

00:38:57.140 --> 00:39:01.040
So, when we only have one
definition of causation

00:39:01.040 --> 00:39:03.330
and we think that's the
definition, et cetera,

00:39:03.330 --> 00:39:05.840
we don't think very much
about the other definition,

00:39:05.840 --> 00:39:09.250
but when we see that
we really have a family

00:39:09.250 --> 00:39:10.850
of several constraints

00:39:10.850 --> 00:39:13.040
and some perhaps are more
fundamental than others.

00:39:13.040 --> 00:39:15.690
Some perhaps are reducible to others

00:39:15.690 --> 00:39:20.690
and some perhaps we tend
to confuse and we shouldn't

00:39:23.170 --> 00:39:25.640
and then there is a lot of work to do.

00:39:25.640 --> 00:39:28.593
So, some of these
relations are well-known.

00:39:29.560 --> 00:39:34.320
For example, cemeteries and
conservation laws are related.

00:39:34.320 --> 00:39:38.120
It was already, people became aware of it

00:39:38.120 --> 00:39:40.590
in the course of the development
of classical mechanics

00:39:40.590 --> 00:39:42.070
and then of course they are the important

00:39:42.070 --> 00:39:44.510
theorems of EMI nature.

00:39:44.510 --> 00:39:48.430
So symmetries and conservation laws

00:39:48.430 --> 00:39:52.240
are closely related, this is well known.

00:39:52.240 --> 00:39:53.420
There are other cases,

00:39:53.420 --> 00:39:58.400
there are things that we seem

00:39:58.400 --> 00:40:01.370
or conceive of as very closely related

00:40:01.370 --> 00:40:03.410
and they are not so closely related.

00:40:03.410 --> 00:40:08.250
For example, determinism and necessity

00:40:08.250 --> 00:40:11.270
or determinism and stability

00:40:11.270 --> 00:40:16.270
or again in more everyday
talk, determinism and fatalism.

00:40:18.780 --> 00:40:19.730
There was a big difference

00:40:19.730 --> 00:40:22.600
of whether one event is very stable

00:40:22.600 --> 00:40:24.160
and no matter where you start,

00:40:24.160 --> 00:40:25.290
you're going to get to it

00:40:25.290 --> 00:40:27.223
or whether we just have determinism.

00:40:31.490 --> 00:40:35.000
And, there is another option also,

00:40:35.000 --> 00:40:37.610
we want to disentangle
determinism and necessity

00:40:37.610 --> 00:40:38.593
and stability.

00:40:39.800 --> 00:40:43.250
This is also a nice case

00:40:43.250 --> 00:40:45.830
where we should disentangle notions.

00:40:45.830 --> 00:40:47.480
Again, in the philosophical literature,

00:40:47.480 --> 00:40:50.040
you'll have a definition of causation

00:40:50.040 --> 00:40:53.323
in terms of actually determinism.

00:40:56.940 --> 00:40:59.930
But then there is a competing definition

00:40:59.930 --> 00:41:03.210
in terms of intervention, right?

00:41:03.210 --> 00:41:05.270
Causal model theories

00:41:05.270 --> 00:41:07.830
are based on this idea of intervention.

00:41:07.830 --> 00:41:09.140
Now, when you think of it,

00:41:09.140 --> 00:41:12.000
when does determinism apply?

00:41:12.000 --> 00:41:14.830
Determinism only applies
in a closed system

00:41:15.930 --> 00:41:20.420
and intervention is always
an intervention from without.

00:41:20.420 --> 00:41:23.400
So, actually there is a tension

00:41:23.400 --> 00:41:26.500
between causation as intervention

00:41:26.500 --> 00:41:30.340
or as manifesting itself
in a case of intervention

00:41:30.340 --> 00:41:32.720
and determinism where
you have a closed system

00:41:32.720 --> 00:41:35.280
and once the initial conditions are given,

00:41:35.280 --> 00:41:36.860
nothing can change.

00:41:36.860 --> 00:41:39.250
And so when you think about

00:41:39.250 --> 00:41:42.935
the different members of this
family of causal constraints,

00:41:42.935 --> 00:41:46.763
you can be more sensitive
to these differences.

00:41:47.680 --> 00:41:51.020
And there are cases, and here
we get to quantum mechanics,

00:41:51.020 --> 00:41:55.180
where there is really a
very fascinating dance

00:41:55.180 --> 00:41:57.540
between different constraints

00:41:57.540 --> 00:42:00.960
and a dance or pair of relation

00:42:00.960 --> 00:42:03.693
that was not evident from the beginning.

00:42:04.790 --> 00:42:09.660
And if we go back to the EPR
situation that we started from,

00:42:09.660 --> 00:42:13.053
which gave rise to the
idea of entanglement,

00:42:14.400 --> 00:42:19.400
here we have a very
peculiar pair of relation

00:42:20.120 --> 00:42:24.000
between determinism and locality

00:42:25.160 --> 00:42:30.160
because it turns out that
the reason we can comply

00:42:32.490 --> 00:42:36.410
with the no signaling constraint

00:42:36.410 --> 00:42:40.310
that gives us compatibility
with special relativity

00:42:40.310 --> 00:42:45.090
is that neither Elise nor Bob

00:42:45.090 --> 00:42:50.090
can predict the results of the
measurement with certainty.

00:42:52.530 --> 00:42:56.170
So it's because when they
look at what they do,

00:42:56.170 --> 00:42:58.290
they cannot infer with certainty

00:42:58.290 --> 00:43:01.500
what the result at the other end was

00:43:01.500 --> 00:43:03.910
that there is no signaling.

00:43:03.910 --> 00:43:05.920
Because if there was determinism,

00:43:05.920 --> 00:43:09.940
they could have used the
correlation that they know

00:43:09.940 --> 00:43:11.470
in order to signal.

00:43:11.470 --> 00:43:14.513
So here we have an example of,

00:43:14.513 --> 00:43:18.970
you think of it, locality
is one constraint

00:43:18.970 --> 00:43:21.420
and it talks about how causal inference

00:43:21.420 --> 00:43:23.320
spreads in space-time,

00:43:23.320 --> 00:43:25.780
which would be continuous, et cetera

00:43:25.780 --> 00:43:26.940
and it would take time

00:43:28.650 --> 00:43:31.190
and determinism is something else.

00:43:31.190 --> 00:43:35.690
It tells us that if we
have the same system again,

00:43:35.690 --> 00:43:38.750
it will develop exactly
the same trajectory

00:43:38.750 --> 00:43:40.130
and on the phase of it,

00:43:40.130 --> 00:43:42.070
we don't see the relation between the two

00:43:42.070 --> 00:43:43.250
and in classical physics

00:43:43.250 --> 00:43:45.980
it's not clear how this relationship works

00:43:45.980 --> 00:43:50.980
but in quantum mechanics, it's
a very intricate relation.

00:43:52.290 --> 00:43:57.110
So, we can now go back

00:44:00.060 --> 00:44:01.990
and we may discuss several questions

00:44:01.990 --> 00:44:05.840
and I want to say something
about the last of these.

00:44:05.840 --> 00:44:07.373
And so, these are not,

00:44:09.710 --> 00:44:12.320
we don't have to discuss them

00:44:12.320 --> 00:44:15.720
but these are just suggestions
for what we could discuss.

00:44:15.720 --> 00:44:18.970
So I asked Elise yesterday

00:44:18.970 --> 00:44:21.360
and I would like to hear more about it,

00:44:21.360 --> 00:44:24.910
how she characterizes her view

00:44:24.910 --> 00:44:27.460
vis-a-vis these other views

00:44:27.460 --> 00:44:31.050
that claim there is no cause of direction

00:44:31.050 --> 00:44:32.700
on the fundamental level of physics,

00:44:32.700 --> 00:44:37.200
in particular there is no causal asymmetry

00:44:37.200 --> 00:44:39.360
at the level of quantum mechanics,

00:44:39.360 --> 00:44:44.040
so if and where this view differs

00:44:44.930 --> 00:44:47.340
because the metaphysical part of the talk,

00:44:47.340 --> 00:44:51.110
I wasn't so sure that I'm following

00:44:51.110 --> 00:44:55.440
the point where you
think these new results

00:44:55.440 --> 00:44:58.990
really make a difference

00:44:58.990 --> 00:45:03.470
to what the other people who
said there is no causal order,

00:45:03.470 --> 00:45:06.120
no temporal order on
the fundamental level.

00:45:06.120 --> 00:45:09.300
I wasn't sure that I
see the exact difference

00:45:09.300 --> 00:45:12.240
between your conclusions,

00:45:12.240 --> 00:45:17.240
from the experiments and from
the theories you reported

00:45:17.340 --> 00:45:21.050
and the point you were trying to make.

00:45:21.050 --> 00:45:22.180
The other question,

00:45:22.180 --> 00:45:23.810
which we didn't discuss yesterday,

00:45:23.810 --> 00:45:25.740
but you mentioned at least

00:45:25.740 --> 00:45:30.660
the possibility of an
epistemic interpretation

00:45:30.660 --> 00:45:32.230
of what we see.

00:45:32.230 --> 00:45:34.610
And I wanted you perhaps,

00:45:34.610 --> 00:45:38.253
if you'd like to expand
a little bit on that,

00:45:39.100 --> 00:45:40.360
would it make a difference?

00:45:40.360 --> 00:45:42.740
What kind of difference would it make?

00:45:42.740 --> 00:45:45.160
Epistemic interpretation
of quantum mechanics

00:45:45.160 --> 00:45:46.790
are quite influential

00:45:47.640 --> 00:45:52.640
and I wonder whether you want
to say a bit more about it,

00:45:54.160 --> 00:45:56.320
I know you mentioned it yesterday.

00:45:56.320 --> 00:45:59.250
The third one is whether free will

00:46:00.880 --> 00:46:05.880
is particularly problematic for any view,

00:46:06.960 --> 00:46:08.640
here I wrote retro-causation,

00:46:08.640 --> 00:46:11.480
but in general, for every view that allows

00:46:11.480 --> 00:46:14.540
an influence from the future to the past.

00:46:14.540 --> 00:46:18.150
I deleted apparently question number four

00:46:18.150 --> 00:46:20.790
because we started to
discuss it yesterday.

00:46:20.790 --> 00:46:25.790
And so the last question to
me is the most troubling one

00:46:27.280 --> 00:46:29.710
and I want to say something about it.

00:46:29.710 --> 00:46:30.610
And the question is

00:46:30.610 --> 00:46:34.470
how does causal order
emerge on higher levels?

00:46:34.470 --> 00:46:37.620
Because we agree that on
higher levels, we do see,

00:46:37.620 --> 00:46:40.340
I suppose you would agree to that

00:46:40.340 --> 00:46:43.520
and we do see temporary order

00:46:43.520 --> 00:46:46.430
and we do or we seem to see,

00:46:46.430 --> 00:46:50.000
we have an impression of temporal
order and of causal order

00:46:50.000 --> 00:46:53.990
and we want to know if not
how they actually emerge,

00:46:53.990 --> 00:46:58.990
how our impression has been formed

00:46:59.670 --> 00:47:02.830
and has become so stable

00:47:02.830 --> 00:47:05.080
that we say, well, it's
our intuition or whatever.

00:47:05.080 --> 00:47:08.670
It has become a very
stable kind of thinking

00:47:08.670 --> 00:47:12.290
that something like that
exists on higher levels.

00:47:12.290 --> 00:47:15.910
And why do I think it's a big question?

00:47:15.910 --> 00:47:20.910
Because we don't know as yet very much

00:47:22.300 --> 00:47:26.180
about the relationship
between quantum mechanics

00:47:26.180 --> 00:47:27.880
and the higher level of physics.

00:47:27.880 --> 00:47:31.700
Let's say quantum mechanics
is the fundamental theory,

00:47:31.700 --> 00:47:32.940
let's forget for a minute

00:47:32.940 --> 00:47:34.530
about general relativity or whatever.

00:47:34.530 --> 00:47:37.900
Let's say quantum mechanics
is the fundamental level,

00:47:37.900 --> 00:47:40.193
on the fundamental level,
there is no causal order,

00:47:40.193 --> 00:47:41.973
there's no temporal order.

00:47:43.170 --> 00:47:44.820
How does it emerge?

00:47:44.820 --> 00:47:46.400
So, I'm not talking about

00:47:46.400 --> 00:47:50.400
why entanglement disappears
in the classical work,

00:47:50.400 --> 00:47:52.130
because there is a good question to that

00:47:52.130 --> 00:47:53.620
and you mentioned it yesterday

00:47:53.620 --> 00:47:56.850
and your work is,

00:47:56.850 --> 00:48:01.850
some of your work goes into
decoherence in great depth.

00:48:02.260 --> 00:48:04.850
So, decoherence explains to us

00:48:04.850 --> 00:48:09.850
why we don't see superposition
in the macro world

00:48:10.620 --> 00:48:11.880
and that's fine,

00:48:11.880 --> 00:48:14.730
but this is not my question
because causal order,

00:48:14.730 --> 00:48:17.340
causal asymmetry and temporal order

00:48:20.300 --> 00:48:23.660
somehow emerge again, either in reality

00:48:23.660 --> 00:48:26.420
or in our perception of reality

00:48:26.420 --> 00:48:28.700
and we want to know how that happens.

00:48:28.700 --> 00:48:33.700
And that brings us back to
their time, thermodynamics

00:48:33.790 --> 00:48:36.900
and answers to that question

00:48:36.900 --> 00:48:40.930
and I think sometimes
there are good arguments

00:48:40.930 --> 00:48:43.450
but very often there is
a lot of hand-waving.

00:48:43.450 --> 00:48:45.110
We live in the macro world

00:48:45.110 --> 00:48:48.460
and here the second law
of thermodynamics applies.

00:48:48.460 --> 00:48:50.190
Well, we know that's not enough.

00:48:50.190 --> 00:48:51.100
So we say yes,

00:48:51.100 --> 00:48:52.950
but our universe is such that

00:48:52.950 --> 00:48:54.700
the initial conditions were such

00:48:54.700 --> 00:48:57.920
and we are the result of this branch.

00:48:57.920 --> 00:48:59.380
We're sitting on that branch

00:48:59.380 --> 00:49:02.610
that started with these
particular initial conditions.

00:49:02.610 --> 00:49:07.610
And, again I'm not sure this
is a very satisfactory answer

00:49:07.810 --> 00:49:12.170
and that it really explains
the mixing of coffee and milk

00:49:12.170 --> 00:49:14.627
as we say in our cappuccinos.

00:49:18.540 --> 00:49:23.540
So, what am I looking for?

00:49:23.780 --> 00:49:25.700
Why am I dissatisfied?

00:49:25.700 --> 00:49:27.840
I want to give one example

00:49:27.840 --> 00:49:32.840
that to me is a paradigm
of a wonderful case

00:49:34.890 --> 00:49:36.750
and that's Feynman's derivation

00:49:36.750 --> 00:49:40.060
and with that I would
conclude, it's my last point.

00:49:40.060 --> 00:49:43.370
And this is the kind of
idea that I want to set

00:49:43.370 --> 00:49:47.350
for explaining the relationship
between quantum mechanics

00:49:47.350 --> 00:49:49.787
or the fundamental level
where there is no causal order

00:49:49.787 --> 00:49:53.120
and the higher levels where
there is a causal order.

00:49:53.120 --> 00:49:57.980
So, Feynman succeeded to recover,

00:49:58.990 --> 00:50:03.470
in recovering the
principle of least action

00:50:03.470 --> 00:50:08.470
from his picture of quantum mechanics.

00:50:08.970 --> 00:50:10.800
And if you think of it,

00:50:10.800 --> 00:50:13.270
there is something very
surprising about that

00:50:13.270 --> 00:50:15.110
because the principle of least action,

00:50:15.110 --> 00:50:16.650
and I said at the beginning,

00:50:16.650 --> 00:50:20.130
variation principles are
very important in physics,

00:50:20.130 --> 00:50:21.770
so there are very, very high up

00:50:21.770 --> 00:50:24.610
in the hierarchy of causal constraints,

00:50:24.610 --> 00:50:27.180
but the principle of least
action and it's likes,

00:50:27.180 --> 00:50:30.450
it's a very strange
principle if you think of it

00:50:30.450 --> 00:50:34.550
because Newtonian
mechanics is okay, right?

00:50:34.550 --> 00:50:38.610
Every particle, every mechanical object

00:50:38.610 --> 00:50:42.110
gets instructions every minute

00:50:42.110 --> 00:50:45.860
and these instructions
determine how it would move.

00:50:45.860 --> 00:50:50.860
But a principle like the
principle of least action

00:50:51.000 --> 00:50:53.713
seems very teleological, right?

00:50:54.560 --> 00:50:59.260
Because how can a system
pick a particular path

00:50:59.260 --> 00:51:04.260
that at each point satisfies
a constraint of economy

00:51:04.430 --> 00:51:07.730
or minimal value to a certain integral.

00:51:07.730 --> 00:51:11.860
It seems that it has to
have the end point in view

00:51:11.860 --> 00:51:14.730
and choose a path that does something

00:51:14.730 --> 00:51:18.797
and this is ridiculous, this
is completely teleological

00:51:18.797 --> 00:51:22.870
and we want to get rid of
this kind of formulation

00:51:22.870 --> 00:51:24.990
in physics, right?

00:51:24.990 --> 00:51:27.113
So, okay, then people prove

00:51:27.113 --> 00:51:29.750
that the principle of least action

00:51:29.750 --> 00:51:31.950
and the formulation of classical mechanics

00:51:31.950 --> 00:51:34.580
in terms of Newtonian
differential equation

00:51:34.580 --> 00:51:36.430
or equations are equivalent

00:51:36.430 --> 00:51:41.430
and we can view it as either
completely causal under Newton,

00:51:42.260 --> 00:51:46.580
or teleological with the
principle of least action

00:51:46.580 --> 00:51:47.800
since our equivalent,

00:51:47.800 --> 00:51:50.830
we don't have to place too much weight

00:51:50.830 --> 00:51:52.683
on either of these perspectives.

00:51:53.610 --> 00:51:55.150
The causal list will say that

00:51:55.150 --> 00:51:58.640
teleological explanation
is a superstructure

00:51:58.640 --> 00:51:59.940
on the causal explanation

00:51:59.940 --> 00:52:03.340
and if someone likes
teleological explanation,

00:52:03.340 --> 00:52:05.910
you would reverse a picture

00:52:05.910 --> 00:52:08.830
and say that the cause of structure

00:52:08.830 --> 00:52:13.143
is a superstructure on the
teleological structure, fine.

00:52:15.450 --> 00:52:17.260
But then comes quantum mechanics

00:52:17.260 --> 00:52:18.800
and it's all probabilities,

00:52:18.800 --> 00:52:20.813
certainly according to Fanno.

00:52:22.110 --> 00:52:25.240
How can we recover the
principle of least action

00:52:25.240 --> 00:52:27.890
with this teleological appearance

00:52:27.890 --> 00:52:32.590
from the underlying chaotic
reality of quantum mechanics

00:52:34.060 --> 00:52:36.420
and Feynman managed to do it

00:52:37.470 --> 00:52:41.140
and there is a calculation,
there is a derivation.

00:52:41.140 --> 00:52:44.060
He had a hint from Dirac

00:52:44.060 --> 00:52:48.280
as to how to define action
in quantum mechanics,

00:52:48.280 --> 00:52:51.433
what is the analog of classical
action in quantum mechanics?

00:52:53.020 --> 00:52:54.830
He was looking for it for a long time

00:52:54.830 --> 00:52:57.150
then he got this hint from a student

00:52:57.150 --> 00:52:59.440
who came over from Europe
and told him about it

00:52:59.440 --> 00:53:03.210
and within hours, as they
say, this is a story,

00:53:03.210 --> 00:53:05.940
he derived the Schrodinger
equation, et cetera, et cetera.

00:53:05.940 --> 00:53:10.040
So he formulated quantum
mechanics in Lagrangian terms

00:53:10.040 --> 00:53:12.052
and in terms of the
principle of least action

00:53:12.052 --> 00:53:16.850
which was his favorite formulation anyway,

00:53:16.850 --> 00:53:19.250
even in work that he did before.

00:53:19.250 --> 00:53:21.480
So this for me is a model

00:53:21.480 --> 00:53:26.480
of deriving something
macroscopic and curious

00:53:27.240 --> 00:53:32.240
and inexplicable on the
phase of it in quantum terms

00:53:33.950 --> 00:53:37.380
and to derive it from the underlying

00:53:37.380 --> 00:53:40.043
reality of quantum mechanics.

00:53:42.180 --> 00:53:43.960
So, what I would like to see,

00:53:43.960 --> 00:53:47.392
not that I'm hoping for
one of us to do it tonight,

00:53:47.392 --> 00:53:49.630
but I think this is a model

00:53:49.630 --> 00:53:52.060
that would not be just hand-waving.

00:53:52.060 --> 00:53:54.350
We sit here in the microscopic world.

00:53:54.350 --> 00:53:55.390
Yes, We sit in microscopic world

00:53:55.390 --> 00:53:57.140
we're worthy of the relation.

00:53:57.140 --> 00:53:59.420
We have to find some continuity

00:53:59.420 --> 00:54:01.210
between this fundamental level,

00:54:01.210 --> 00:54:03.140
which there is no causal order

00:54:03.140 --> 00:54:05.343
and the higher levels on which there is.

00:54:06.200 --> 00:54:08.860
So, any of these points

00:54:08.860 --> 00:54:12.830
or other points that you
want to react to Elise,

00:54:12.830 --> 00:54:14.073
I'd be delighted.

00:54:16.530 --> 00:54:18.900
- Well, thank you so much Yemima.

00:54:18.900 --> 00:54:20.700
There's just so much goodness there.

00:54:21.580 --> 00:54:24.200
I wrote notes and notes and
notes while you were speaking.

00:54:24.200 --> 00:54:26.010
So I will say very briefly

00:54:26.010 --> 00:54:29.640
and then sort of respond
to a couple of things

00:54:29.640 --> 00:54:31.440
and then let others weigh in

00:54:31.440 --> 00:54:33.080
and we can see where the discussion goes,

00:54:33.080 --> 00:54:35.310
what people find most interesting.

00:54:35.310 --> 00:54:36.870
But the first thing I wanna say is that

00:54:36.870 --> 00:54:39.230
I find your definition of causality

00:54:39.230 --> 00:54:41.220
as a constraint times change,

00:54:41.220 --> 00:54:43.870
to be I think among the
most robust definitions

00:54:43.870 --> 00:54:44.703
I've encountered

00:54:44.703 --> 00:54:47.720
where causality is not
at the fundamental level.

00:54:47.720 --> 00:54:49.870
Indeed constraints are not,

00:54:49.870 --> 00:54:52.020
they're sort of meta properties, right?

00:54:52.020 --> 00:54:55.090
They're properties about
how properties can be

00:54:55.090 --> 00:54:57.900
and I find it extraordinarily helpful

00:54:57.900 --> 00:54:59.900
and so I really like it.

00:54:59.900 --> 00:55:01.090
But what I think

00:55:01.090 --> 00:55:05.600
indefinite causal ordering
experiments are doing

00:55:05.600 --> 00:55:09.100
is putting to test what
happens when we drop

00:55:09.100 --> 00:55:14.100
a sort of our, what we assume
to be the correct constraints

00:55:14.180 --> 00:55:19.180
for the loose ordering of certain events

00:55:19.300 --> 00:55:21.020
or certain operations

00:55:21.020 --> 00:55:24.600
and what they find is actual fruit, right?

00:55:24.600 --> 00:55:25.740
Not just that,

00:55:25.740 --> 00:55:30.740
not just greater efficiency in
quantum computation perhaps,

00:55:31.080 --> 00:55:33.090
but there's also a suggestion that

00:55:33.090 --> 00:55:34.480
it may guide how we think

00:55:34.480 --> 00:55:36.230
about quantization of gravity

00:55:37.370 --> 00:55:39.430
by thinking about these things.

00:55:39.430 --> 00:55:42.730
So, in a way, and somebody
had asked yesterday,

00:55:42.730 --> 00:55:46.810
I think it was Fext or maybe it was Peter,

00:55:46.810 --> 00:55:48.680
how I moved from decoherence to this.

00:55:48.680 --> 00:55:50.480
Well decoherence is exactly

00:55:50.480 --> 00:55:52.790
the rich studies that come out of that

00:55:52.790 --> 00:55:54.730
are based on realizing that

00:55:54.730 --> 00:55:56.750
we only do quantum mechanics
in the first place,

00:55:56.750 --> 00:55:58.470
as you said Yemima,

00:55:58.470 --> 00:56:00.610
by invoking certain assumptions

00:56:00.610 --> 00:56:03.410
or idealizations about the
closeness and isolatedness

00:56:05.090 --> 00:56:07.310
of the system of interest

00:56:07.310 --> 00:56:09.490
that we can carve the
world up in certain ways

00:56:09.490 --> 00:56:12.157
and this is the apparatus and
this is the system of interest

00:56:12.157 --> 00:56:13.810
and there's the environment

00:56:13.810 --> 00:56:16.040
and we can neglect the
environment and so on,

00:56:16.040 --> 00:56:17.910
and we can pretend that
Schrodinger's equation

00:56:17.910 --> 00:56:20.500
really univocally evolves, right?

00:56:20.500 --> 00:56:22.266
And unitarily, right?

00:56:22.266 --> 00:56:23.560
Univocally and unitarily.

00:56:23.560 --> 00:56:26.060
So there's deterministic aspects there,

00:56:26.060 --> 00:56:27.890
but that's not true.

00:56:27.890 --> 00:56:29.740
Anytime you have an interaction,

00:56:29.740 --> 00:56:34.143
you have non-unitary terms introduced.

00:56:35.672 --> 00:56:38.320
So I think, even if I
agree with you totally,

00:56:38.320 --> 00:56:41.270
which I do about this
definition of causation,

00:56:41.270 --> 00:56:42.760
there's two things,

00:56:42.760 --> 00:56:44.483
the one is that, well,

00:56:45.480 --> 00:56:50.010
one of the arguments
for maintaining a robust

00:56:50.010 --> 00:56:53.270
understanding of causality
at higher levels,

00:56:53.270 --> 00:56:56.270
and I take it that you said you disagree

00:56:56.270 --> 00:56:59.370
that there is no causal
principle you think, right?

00:56:59.370 --> 00:57:01.370
No universal causal principle,

00:57:01.370 --> 00:57:04.710
I think we agree about that,
that seems pretty clear.

00:57:04.710 --> 00:57:06.939
The question then is, okay,

00:57:06.939 --> 00:57:10.650
how do we get usefulness
for the clearly useful,

00:57:10.650 --> 00:57:14.083
clearly fruitful higher level concepts,

00:57:15.490 --> 00:57:16.450
but at the same time,

00:57:16.450 --> 00:57:20.440
the fact that it is by dropping
the concept of causality,

00:57:20.440 --> 00:57:25.270
by dropping a presupposed
background dependence

00:57:25.270 --> 00:57:27.153
on a particular structure.

00:57:28.780 --> 00:57:31.590
So for instance, change.

00:57:31.590 --> 00:57:34.590
Change requires that you
parse up the phase space

00:57:34.590 --> 00:57:35.890
in a certain way

00:57:35.890 --> 00:57:39.250
and look at how the system
moves through contiguous cells

00:57:39.250 --> 00:57:40.650
in that phase space.

00:57:40.650 --> 00:57:42.393
So ICO, I take it,

00:57:43.640 --> 00:57:44.750
people investigating this

00:57:44.750 --> 00:57:47.200
are trying to develop theories that

00:57:47.200 --> 00:57:51.290
do not presuppose any
parsing of phase space

00:57:51.290 --> 00:57:52.930
or any parsing of that space.

00:57:52.930 --> 00:57:54.720
So they still measure change

00:57:54.720 --> 00:57:58.980
but the change they're
measuring is sort of relative.

00:57:58.980 --> 00:58:00.100
It isn't an ultimate,

00:58:00.100 --> 00:58:03.320
it isn't based in some
structure of the world

00:58:03.320 --> 00:58:07.380
that's truly causal or has true
different split time points,

00:58:07.380 --> 00:58:10.620
which is exactly what
Einstein, as you mentioned,

00:58:10.620 --> 00:58:15.620
why he rejected Newton's
absolute space-time cathedral,

00:58:16.340 --> 00:58:18.650
or the theater in which
these events happened.

00:58:18.650 --> 00:58:22.100
He wanted to drop that absolute background

00:58:22.100 --> 00:58:25.950
and let the laws of physics
sort of work within that

00:58:25.950 --> 00:58:30.240
and I think that that's
a similar question here.

00:58:30.240 --> 00:58:31.360
So that's the first thing

00:58:31.360 --> 00:58:34.820
is that thinking about causal
constraints could be useful,

00:58:34.820 --> 00:58:38.370
but also how we can get
around those constraints

00:58:38.370 --> 00:58:42.120
or untether the way those constraints

00:58:42.120 --> 00:58:46.520
are more to particular
parsings of space and time,

00:58:46.520 --> 00:58:48.820
because that's where we get change,

00:58:48.820 --> 00:58:50.980
could be very fruitful too.

00:58:50.980 --> 00:58:52.610
So that's that point

00:58:52.610 --> 00:58:55.810
and then I'll end with
a metaphysical point,

00:58:55.810 --> 00:58:57.833
which is again, constraints,

00:58:59.500 --> 00:59:02.660
they can't be primitive, can they?

00:59:02.660 --> 00:59:07.120
I mean, our constraints of
brute fact of the world,

00:59:07.120 --> 00:59:09.810
it seems they couldn't be
because we have counterexamples

00:59:09.810 --> 00:59:11.730
like spontaneous symmetry breaking,

00:59:11.730 --> 00:59:16.130
or I don't know, parity,
failure in beta decay.

00:59:16.130 --> 00:59:19.030
It seems that our symmetries

00:59:19.030 --> 00:59:20.650
and there's a lot of
literature about this,

00:59:20.650 --> 00:59:24.320
which I'm not totally
up on and you may be,

00:59:24.320 --> 00:59:27.410
but symmetry isn't gonna be
our guiding light either.

00:59:27.410 --> 00:59:29.010
And there is something-

00:59:29.010 --> 00:59:30.383
- They are or they are not?

00:59:31.220 --> 00:59:33.169
Sorry, they are going to be

00:59:33.169 --> 00:59:36.740
or they're not going to be our guidelines?

00:59:36.740 --> 00:59:41.500
- They cannot be, I think,
because of these counterexamples.

00:59:41.500 --> 00:59:43.720
So they are very useful heuristics,

00:59:43.720 --> 00:59:45.570
they are very important for modeling

00:59:45.570 --> 00:59:47.760
and even for developing new theories

00:59:47.760 --> 00:59:50.710
but they're useful as guides

00:59:50.710 --> 00:59:53.987
but problematic is dogma, one might say,

00:59:53.987 --> 00:59:56.050
and if you were talking about

00:59:56.050 --> 00:59:57.790
what does the world really look like?

00:59:57.790 --> 00:59:59.020
I think constraints,

00:59:59.020 --> 01:00:01.230
you either have to accept
them as a brute fact,

01:00:01.230 --> 01:00:02.750
the world just as the way that

01:00:02.750 --> 01:00:05.080
such that momentum is conserved,

01:00:05.080 --> 01:00:08.370
or you say, there's
something deeper going on.

01:00:08.370 --> 01:00:11.750
And in this way, maybe the debate here

01:00:11.750 --> 01:00:15.000
is a little bit like Brown
and Pooley and others

01:00:15.000 --> 01:00:16.380
with their Lorentzian pedagogy.

01:00:16.380 --> 01:00:18.600
It's like what do you
count as a brute fact?

01:00:18.600 --> 01:00:21.380
Is it Lorentzian variants
that's a brute fact of universe

01:00:21.380 --> 01:00:23.310
or is it the way the metric is?

01:00:23.310 --> 01:00:26.080
And that sort of
explanatory story you tell

01:00:26.080 --> 01:00:28.060
about how physics,

01:00:28.060 --> 01:00:31.070
how the other theories
sort of rely on that

01:00:31.070 --> 01:00:33.130
under that underpinning,

01:00:33.130 --> 01:00:36.030
depends on what you want
to accept as a brute fact

01:00:36.030 --> 01:00:37.970
in your metaphysic.

01:00:37.970 --> 01:00:39.820
So, yeah.

01:00:39.820 --> 01:00:41.420
Thank you.

01:00:41.420 --> 01:00:42.970
And I wanna think a lot more

01:00:42.970 --> 01:00:46.100
about the retro-causality
thing, I hadn't yet.

01:00:46.100 --> 01:00:49.380
I had taken Hue's work to be sort of

01:00:50.697 --> 01:00:53.590
not directly targeting the asymmetry,

01:00:53.590 --> 01:00:55.680
but if he is targeting
the asymmetry argument,

01:00:55.680 --> 01:00:59.730
then I'm clearly aligned in
some ways with what he's up to.

01:00:59.730 --> 01:01:03.160
So I will have to take a deeper
dive into that literature.

01:01:03.160 --> 01:01:03.993
Thank you.

01:01:06.250 --> 01:01:08.540
- Great. Thank you so much.

01:01:08.540 --> 01:01:10.980
- Can I have a quick(indistinct).

01:01:10.980 --> 01:01:12.800
- Yeah, sure, please go ahead, yeah.

01:01:12.800 --> 01:01:16.353
- Yeah, well, I think
I try to make it clear

01:01:16.353 --> 01:01:20.273
that I don't see any of
these in a dogmatic way.

01:01:20.273 --> 01:01:23.240
I don't see any of these is a priori.

01:01:23.240 --> 01:01:28.240
I think our knowledge
of the world develops

01:01:29.500 --> 01:01:34.120
and our notions, our explanatory
notions develope with it.

01:01:34.120 --> 01:01:36.790
We didn't have a notion
like general covariance

01:01:36.790 --> 01:01:40.000
before Einstein's general
theory of relativity

01:01:40.000 --> 01:01:42.270
and then we have this notion.

01:01:42.270 --> 01:01:44.800
And what is beneficial?

01:01:44.800 --> 01:01:48.100
So I'm not dogmatic about any of these

01:01:48.100 --> 01:01:51.150
and of course we learn
that some laws do not apply

01:01:51.150 --> 01:01:52.240
and if you only think about

01:01:52.240 --> 01:01:55.160
the history of the conservation of energy,

01:01:55.160 --> 01:01:58.810
so his topic was mass has then developing

01:01:58.810 --> 01:02:01.607
and we don't know now what is dark matter

01:02:01.607 --> 01:02:03.360
but I can tell us more about it.

01:02:03.360 --> 01:02:06.770
So we don't know where
conservation of mass energy

01:02:06.770 --> 01:02:08.940
would lead us in the future.

01:02:08.940 --> 01:02:12.580
These are open things in our
eye, it's an open texture.

01:02:12.580 --> 01:02:14.120
Science is an open texture

01:02:14.120 --> 01:02:16.360
and our causal notions are open texture

01:02:16.360 --> 01:02:18.880
and perhaps we'll drop
some of the constraints.

01:02:18.880 --> 01:02:21.080
Perhaps we have dropped determinism.

01:02:21.080 --> 01:02:22.250
I mentioned determinism,

01:02:22.250 --> 01:02:24.220
but it's not that I think that determinism

01:02:24.220 --> 01:02:26.270
is still an accepted constraint.

01:02:26.270 --> 01:02:29.340
So sometimes we benefit
from adding constraints

01:02:29.340 --> 01:02:31.870
like when Einstein said,

01:02:31.870 --> 01:02:36.640
I don't like it that matter
doesn't act back on space.

01:02:36.640 --> 01:02:39.460
Let me add this constraint,
it should act back.

01:02:39.460 --> 01:02:41.530
Then it has dynamical space-time

01:02:41.530 --> 01:02:44.020
and background independence and wonderful.

01:02:44.020 --> 01:02:47.250
So he added the constraint
or general covariance.

01:02:47.250 --> 01:02:50.110
On the other hand, we gain a
lot from dropping constraints,

01:02:50.110 --> 01:02:51.980
like dropping determinism.

01:02:51.980 --> 01:02:55.010
Some people say drop compatibility

01:02:55.010 --> 01:02:56.840
with special relativity, whatever.

01:02:56.840 --> 01:02:58.910
So it can work in either way.

01:02:58.910 --> 01:03:02.730
And the last thing I wanted
is to be dogmatic about it

01:03:02.730 --> 01:03:03.940
but to the contrary,

01:03:03.940 --> 01:03:06.100
I want you to say there
is no one definition

01:03:06.100 --> 01:03:09.010
of causation that a philosopher can give.

01:03:09.010 --> 01:03:12.400
It's the way science develops the terrace.

01:03:12.400 --> 01:03:14.000
Now, whether it's a brute fact,

01:03:14.000 --> 01:03:16.130
I don't know what brute facts are,

01:03:16.130 --> 01:03:17.930
unless you want individual fact.

01:03:17.930 --> 01:03:22.240
There is what they call in
philosophy, human supervenience.

01:03:22.240 --> 01:03:24.830
All the reason the word are the track.

01:03:24.830 --> 01:03:27.840
And then there are our
most economical ways

01:03:27.840 --> 01:03:29.040
of summarizing up these.

01:03:32.090 --> 01:03:35.700
I'm not sure that all laws of nature

01:03:35.700 --> 01:03:39.890
like conservation of energy, for example,

01:03:39.890 --> 01:03:43.390
although it is, as I said,

01:03:43.390 --> 01:03:45.807
the future of this law is open.

01:03:45.807 --> 01:03:48.220
I don't think we understand them

01:03:48.220 --> 01:03:50.370
as a summary of what happened.

01:03:50.370 --> 01:03:51.330
We want to say,

01:03:51.330 --> 01:03:54.190
don't invest in building a
perpetual mobile machine,

01:03:54.190 --> 01:03:55.170
it won't work.

01:03:56.030 --> 01:03:58.220
There is some kind of
stronger necessities,

01:03:58.220 --> 01:04:00.103
and just the summary of the facts.

01:04:01.590 --> 01:04:06.590
So I think constraints are viewed by us

01:04:06.660 --> 01:04:08.620
in a different way than facts,

01:04:08.620 --> 01:04:10.740
but it doesn't mean that
they're not fundamental.

01:04:10.740 --> 01:04:12.430
I don't know of anything more fundamental

01:04:12.430 --> 01:04:15.010
in physics than some basic symmetries,

01:04:15.010 --> 01:04:17.010
but it's all the feasible.

01:04:17.010 --> 01:04:19.233
Tomorrow we may prove wrong.

01:04:23.570 --> 01:04:24.720
